[al:新概念英语第三册]
[ti:24. A Skeleton in the Cupboard]
[00:00.520] Passage 24. | 文章24。
[00:02.055] A skeleton in the cupboard. | 橱柜里的骷髅。
[00:05.070] We often read in novels how a seemingly respectable person or family | 我们经常在小说中读到，一个看似体面的人或家庭
[00:10.770] has some terrible secret which has been concealed from strangers for years. | 都有一些可怕的秘密，多年来一直对陌生人隐瞒。
[00:15.820] The English language possesses a vivid saying to describe this sort of situation. | 英语中有一个生动的说法来描述这种情况。
[00:21.140] The terrible secret is called a skeleton in the cupboard. | 这个可怕的秘密被称为“橱柜里的骷髅”。
[00:25.040] At some dramatic moment in the story, | 在故事的某个戏剧性时刻，
[00:27.660] the terrible secret becomes known and a reputation is ruined. | 这个可怕的秘密被公之于众，名誉扫地。
[00:31.935] The reader's hair stands on end when he reads in the final pages of the novel | 当读者在小说的最后一页读到
[00:36.995] that the heroine, a dear old lady, | 女主人公——一位亲爱的老太太——
[00:39.620] who had always been so kind to everybody, | 总是对每个人都那么好，
[00:42.210] had in her youth poisoned every one of her five husbands. | 在她年轻时毒死了她的五个丈夫时，会感到毛骨悚然。
[00:46.580] It is all very well for such things to occur in fiction. | 这种事情发生在小说里是完全可以接受的。
[00:50.785] To varying degrees, we all have secrets | 在不同程度上，我们都有秘密，
[00:54.044] which we do not want even our closest friends to learn. | 甚至不想让最亲密的朋友知道。
[00:57.150] But few of us have skeletons in the cupboard. | 但我们中很少有人有“橱柜里的骷髅”。
[01:00.130] The only person I know who has a skeleton in the cupboard is George Carlton, | 我认识的唯一一个有“橱柜里的骷髅”的人是乔治·卡尔顿，
[01:05.395] and he is very proud of the fact. | 他对此感到非常自豪。
[01:07.950] George studied medicine in his youth. | 乔治年轻时学过医。
[01:10.945] Instead of becoming a doctor, however, | 然而，他没有成为一名医生，
[01:13.495] he became a successful writer of detective stories. | 而是成为了一名成功的侦探小说作家。
[01:17.300] I once spent an uncomfortable weekend which I shall never forget at his house. | 我曾在他家度过一个我永远不会忘记的不愉快的周末。
[01:22.395] George showed me to the guest room, | 乔治把我带到客房，
[01:24.840] which he said was rarely used. | 他说那个房间很少用。
[01:27.350] He told me to unpack my things and then come down to dinner. | 他叫我收拾好行李，然后下来吃晚饭。
[01:31.910] After I had stacked my shirts and underclothes in two empty drawers, | 我把衬衫和内衣叠放在两个空抽屉里后，
[01:36.910] I decided to hang in the cupboard one of the two suits I had brought with me. | 决定把带来的两套西装中的一套挂进衣柜。
[01:41.600] I opened the cupboard door | 我打开衣柜门，
[01:43.980] and then stood in front of it petrified. | 然后吓呆地站在它前面。
[01:47.615] A skeleton was dangling before my eyes. | 一个骷髅在我眼前晃动。
[01:51.440] The sudden movement of the door made it sway slightly, | 门的突然移动使它轻轻摇晃，
[01:54.765] and it gave me the impression that it was about to leap out at me. | 给我一种它要扑向我的感觉。
[01:58.485] Dropping my suit, I dashed downstairs to tell George. | 我扔下西装，冲下楼去告诉乔治。
[02:02.640] This was worse than a terrible secret, | 这比一个可怕的秘密更糟糕，
[02:05.300] this was a real skeleton. | 这是一个真正的骷髅。
[02:07.820] But George was unsympathetic. | 但乔治却不以为然。
[02:11.235] "Oh, that," he said with a smile, | “哦，那个啊，”他微笑着说，
[02:14.185] as if he were talking about an old friend. | 好像在谈论一位老朋友。
[02:16.960] "That's Sebastian." | “那是塞巴斯蒂安。”
[02:19.260] "You forget that I was a medical student once upon a time." | “你忘了我以前也是医学生。”